Gonorrhoea
Young Adult & Adolecence
Copious purulent discharge, Severe dysuria, Positive NAAT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-020
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe dysuria and copious purulent urethral discharge causing immense discomfort and anxiety regarding rapid symptom escalation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a hyperacute onset of severe, burning dysuria accompanied by a continuous, profuse purulent discharge. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was necessitated to rapidly exclude ascending epididymo-orchitis or systemic dissemination, and to administer immediate, potent empiric intramuscular and oral antimicrobial therapy to halt transmission and prevent irreversible genitourinary tissue damage prior to morning clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It feels like I am peeing broken glass, and there is thick, yellow fluid constantly leaking out of me."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 24-hour history of an abruptly onset, intensely painful genitourinary syndrome. The primary symptom is severe dysuria, characterized as a burning, sharp pain during micturition. This is accompanied by a continuous, copious purulent urethral discharge that soils the patient's undergarments. The patient denies swollen or tender testicles, but reports mild lower abdominal heaviness. There is no exudative pharyngitis, anal pruritus, or tenesmus, ruling out extragenital mucosal involvement at this time. The rapid, aggressive onset of the thick, purulent discharge is the primary driver of the acute distress.
Functional Status: Ambulating, but actively avoiding urination due to the severe anticipatory pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is distressed about the severity of the discharge and the immediate implications for their sexual health and recent partners.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 90 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears flushed, anxious, and uncomfortable.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary examination (chaperoned) reveals an erythematous, oedematous urethral meatus. There is an active, spontaneous, copious purulent (thick, yellow-green) discharge easily visualised. Scrotal palpation demonstrates normal, non-tender testes and epididymides, specifically ruling out acute epididymo-orchitis. Abdominal exam is soft and non-tender. Joint examination reveals no migratory polyarthralgia, and tendons show no tenosynovitis, effectively excluding disseminated gonococcal infection. The clinical phenotype strongly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of copious purulent discharge and severe dysuria, urgently mandating a first-catch urine for multiplex NAAT to confirm the presence of Neisseria gonorrhoeae.
Environmental Observations: Tissues and soiled undergarments are present in the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Uncomplicated Gonococcal Urethritis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Chlamydia Trachomatis Urethritis - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary pathogen, given the discharge in Chlamydia is typically scant and mucopurulent (clear/white), whereas this presentation is hyperacute, profuse, and frankly purulent (yellow/green).

Trichomonas Vaginalis (in males) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the extreme severity of the dysuria and the sheer volume of the purulent discharge, which is atypical for the usually mild or asymptomatic presentation of Trichomonas in men.

Acute Epididymitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely normal, non-tender scrotal examination, localizing the severe inflammation entirely to the anterior urethra.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the hallmark hyperacute signs of a gonococcal infection: severe, burning dysuria and a continuous, profuse purulent urethral discharge. The severity of the mucosal inflammation and the high risk of ascending infection or rapid transmission required an immediate after-hours home evaluation. Delaying assessment until a routine appointment would have prolonged severe pain, increased the risk of the infection ascending to cause epididymo-orchitis, and delayed the crucial epidemiological step of halting transmission. Immediate clinical examination safely excluded systemic dissemination and allowed for the immediate administration of dual-therapy empiric antibiotics to rapidly eradicate the pathogen.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ceftriaxone 500mg IM stat (to cover N. gonorrhoeae) and prescribed Doxycycline 100mg PO BID for 7 days (to provide empiric dual coverage for frequently co-occurring Chlamydia). Collected a first-catch urine sample to be sent in the morning for multiplex nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT).
Positive Behavioral Change: To facilitate urethral healing and prevent transmitting the infection, maintain absolute abstinence from all sexual contact for the next 7 days, directly removing the friction that worsens pain and breaking the chain of infection. To ensure the bacteria are completely destroyed, take the oral Doxycycline consistently twice a day until finished, explicitly recognizing that feeling better does not mean the bacteria are entirely dead.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 3 days to review the NAAT results, ensure complete symptom resolution, and initiate formal contact tracing for recent sexual partners.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the patient develops swollen, agonizingly painful testicles, a high fever with shivering, or multiple painful, swollen joints across the body.

